// An initializer must name a field the type declares (E0005), decided by the checker wherever the
// literal names its type. Whether a written name is a field is settled by the declaration alone, so
// nothing about the literal's other parts can change the answer.
//
// That is what separates this rule from the completeness rule beside it. E0009 asks which fields end
// up *filled*, which a `...base` spread of a `dyn` value only answers at construction
// (`literal_missing_field_dynamic.noe`), so that one defers. E0005 asks which names are *declared*,
// and a spread can only fill declared fields, never introduce a name — so the second literal below
// is refused statically even though its sibling rule cannot be.
//
// Every literal sits in a function nothing calls, so the rejection can only be the checker's: a
// program that reached its runtime would print `alive` and exit 0, and both expectations below would
// fail. That is what keeps this apart from the same code raised at the same span by a backend as it
// builds the object.
// expect: error E0005 at 27:26
// expect: error E0005 at 31:26
// expect: error E0005 at 35:31
// expect: error E0005 at 40:21
// expect: exit 1
struct Point { x: int  y: int }
struct Flat { x: int }
struct Marker { }

fn opaque(): dyn { return Flat { x: 1 }; }

fn misnamed(): Point {
    return Point { x: 1, z: 2, y: 3 };
}

fn misnamed_under_a_spread(): Point {
    return Point { y: 2, z: 9, ...opaque() };
}

fn misnamed_target_typed(): Point {
    p: Point = .{ x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };
    return p;
}

fn misnamed_on_a_fieldless_type(): Marker {
    return Marker { z: 1 };
}

echo "alive";
